    <h2>Disagreement is no agreement at all</h2>\
    <p>At 11:43am I was wondering why I cared so much about ancient philosophy. Why do I care about Platonism and Stoicism and Epicureanism? Why do I spend my time thinking about which one is "more true." Not "true" or "completely true", just "more true." And that should tell you something about why, but for now it didn't tell me enough about why.</p>\
\
    <p>I don't have the answer to that question, but I have been thinking about the game of verification. By this I mean the process of verifying which claim is true, the Epicurean one or the Stoic one. It's a fun exercise, like a heady sudoku; and maybe it's even handy. ancient philosophy can provide distant analogies to juxtapose with any present situation. the juxtaposition rarely "solves" anything but it can get you unstuck from the present a bit. Getting unstuck is not nothing. What this exercise needs is an impetus not only to confirm a correct proposition but to destroy the world that created the bad one\'97 not just the incorrect one but the bad one.</p>\
\
    <p>As I write this I'm noticing that my parallel syntax is out of joint and that makes sense (it's perfectly brilliant of me, actually [which is to say, accidentally]) because the grammar of the game is out of joint. You can infinitely disprove false statements and prove true statements but this does little to stop or change the machine (be it person or algorithm or algorithmic person) who generate them. We see this all the time. It's the news cycle. It's the timeline. It's the two-party system. They're engaged in an infinite tennis match, an unending conversation, and the rules of this game enable them to keep playing it and never stop. These rules have to be replaced. The old game must be destroyed by the emergence of a new game, a better game. That is the impetus that goes beyond idle speculation and into the realms of delibration and creation.</p>\
\
    <p>Preferably, that impetus is also affective (full of joy or hatred). So the exercise is not purely intellectual but socially, emotionally, spiritual, materially important.</p>\
\
    <p>Ultimately, from this meditation, a practical rule emerged for me to orient with some ongoing projects: [[My response to Platonism or Stoicism cannot simply oppose the material to the immaterial, and it cannot respond to negation with affirmation.]]</p>\
\
    <p>An example of this is how [[Deleuze takes Epicurean atomism as a conceptual method for formalizing problems in philosophy, not physics.]] Deleuze does not oppose the atomism of Epicureanism, nor does he accept it. He works within its functions in order to apply them to another context. He applies a method of transduction. Not opposition, not confirmation, but affirmation by means of transduction. Reserve agreement and disagreement for the realm of argumentation and opinion. Leave them to argue, and while they argue go make something for yourself that actually (or accidentally) works.</p>\
\
    <h2>Rogue-like Line Crawler</h2>\
    <ul>\
        <li>Random line from unfinished work: Why do we need "critical thinking"? Because we misunderstand how we use common sense.</li>\
    </ul>\
\
    <p>Random quote (insert "Telemaque" with any book. Really. Any book):</p>\
    <blockquote>\
        <p>Does the student want to learn how to read? Does he want to learn English or German, the art of litigation or of combat? The madman, imperturbably, will put a copy of Telemaque in his hands and the student will begin to repeat, "Calypso," "Calypso could", "Calypso could not," and so on \'85 There was only one rule: he must be able to show, in the book, the materiality of everything he says. He will he asked to write compositions and perform improvisations under the same conditions: he must use the words and turns of phrase in the book to construct his sentences; he must show, in the book, the facts on which his reasoning is based. In short, the master must be able to verify in the book the materiality of everything the student says. (Ranciere, Ignorant Schoolmaster, p. 20)</p>\
    </blockquote>\
\
